<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Teusina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Tyavzino · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-swedish-war-1590-1595">Russo-Swedish War (1590–1595)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Teusina was signed on 18 May 1595 at Teusina, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, ending the Russo-Swedish War of 1590–1595. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised Swedish possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> returned the fortress and district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a> to Russia. The treaty also set out a border between the two states in the far north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-teusina.jpg" alt="Treaty of Teusina" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ximfel1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Border_changes_in_Finland_1595.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had agreed a truce in 1593 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden">John III of Sweden</a>. The Swedes sought peace because they had lost their king and feared for their army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, which lacked funds. Sigismund was named the new king, but Sweden soon fell into a power struggle between Sigismund and his uncle, the future Charles IX. In Russia Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-i-of-russia">Fyodor I Ivanovich</a> reigned, while real power lay with his adviser and later successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>.</p><p>Sigismund set as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s minimum demand a return to the pre-war borders with some territorial compensation. The Russian delegation, led by the boyar Afanasiy Pushkin, used the Swedish quarrel to its advantage and threatened to break off the talks, and the final agreement was reached in May 1595 at the village of Tyavzino, Täyssinä in Finnish, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty revised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-plussa">Truce of Plussa</a> of 1583 and restored the borders that had stood before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>. Sweden gave up the occupied lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> received Kexholm County with Korela Fortress and most of Ingria, with the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, Yama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a>. Russia renounced all claims west of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> and accepted Swedish sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Prisoners were exchanged without ransom, and the safety of Russian subjects and their property was guaranteed. The border was traced from the mouth of the Systerbäck on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> past the lakes Saimaa and Inari and the settlement of Neiden to the Barents Sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peace ended twenty-five years of destructive war, in which tens of thousands of civilians had been killed, at that time a much larger portion of the population of the regions where the war was fought. Its provisions on Russian foreign trade, which was to pass through Swedish ports, favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> commercially. The borders it set lasted 22 years, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> in 1617.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Teusina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sweden held on through the long Livonian War, and the <b>Treaty of Teusina</b> in 1595 confirmed its possession of northern Estonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1561-1721">Duchy of Estonia (1561-1721)</a></li></ul></section>
